\paragraph{Position encoding mediates the V-shape recovery.}
The monotonic L2 curve versus the non-monotonic random curve on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long (Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}) reflects an interaction between token selection strategy and positional encoding. L2 norm scoring preserves spatial locality: retained tokens form near-contiguous clusters in the ViT grid, producing continuous position shifts after re-indexing that Gemma's RoPE accommodates smoothly---hence monotonic degradation (86.8\% $\to$ 50.2\% from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.5$). Random selection creates scattered gaps that produce position aliasing after re-indexing. At $k{\approx}0.9$, the aliasing is maximally destructive: a small number of randomly placed gaps create highly irregular inter-token spacing, collapsing SR to 31.8\%. At $k{=}0.7$, enough tokens are removed that the surviving positions become approximately uniform again, and SR recovers to 64.6\%---exceeding the $k{=}0.9$ level by $2{\times}$. The zero-mask ablation (Section~\ref{sec:zeromask}) provides causal confirmation: zeroing token content while preserving positions yields 98.0\% SR, while physically removing the same tokens collapses to 9.0\%. Position encoding, not information content, is the binding constraint---and scoring method determines whether that constraint is violated.

